  <title>Welcome To A New Branch Of Quantum Computing!</title>
  <dc:creator>NicDeclic </dc:creator>
  <link>https://nicdeclic.com/posts/welcome/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p><img src="https://nicdeclic.com/images/splash.jpg" class="img-fluid"></p>
<div class="text-end">
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p><em>“Out of the void arises matter, just as out of silence emerges an idea.”</em><br>
— <strong>Professor Alvin</strong></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<hr>
<section id="welcome-fellow-explorers" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="welcome-fellow-explorers">Welcome, Fellow Explorers!</h3>
<p>Whether you are a seasoned quantum physicist, a curious software engineer, a student wandering through the fringes of the web, or simply someone fascinated by the architecture of reality: <strong>welcome aboard.</strong></p>
<p>You have just stepped into an open laboratory dedicated to an unconventional scientific journey. Here, we step away from traditional silicon chips and ultra-cold cryostats to explore how the elegant, subtle geometry of conjugated carbon systems could form the backbone of a radically different kind of quantum processor. Make yourself comfortable, bring your curiosity, and let’s explore what happens when we look at matter through the lens of computation.</p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="how-i-work" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="how-i-work">How I Work</h3>
<p>The bulk of this research has been conducted over the years using nothing more than a simple graphite pencil and stacks of paper. I find it deeply amusing that the very same carbon atoms I smear across a sheet of paper might one day, if arranged with enough precision, form a quantum supercomputer.</p>
<p>This website is the digital transcription of my physical laboratory notebook, and I will continue adding new findings as time permits. It is an evolving work in progress.</p>
<p>Scientific inquiry consists of sudden bursts of euphoria followed by long, quiet stretches of rumination. Do not be discouraged if updates pause for long intervals; that downtime is simply the recharge cycle my neurons require before taking the next quantum leap.</p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="a-disclaimer-on-the-speculation-wild-thoughts-section" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-disclaimer-on-the-speculation-wild-thoughts-section">A Disclaimer on the Speculation / Wild Thoughts Section</h3>
<p>The creative phase of scientific discovery is often anything but strictly scientific. True inspiration can strike from the most unexpected corners of reality.</p>
<p>The <strong>Speculations</strong> section at the bottom of each article exists so I can think out loud without self-censorship. Any idea is welcome there, provided it has a conceptual thread connecting it to the topic at hand — and above all, the spark needed to ignite novel ways of thinking.</p>
<p><em>A fair warning:</em> If your sensibilities are offended by free-form intuition that casually weaves rigorous science, spaghetti sauce, space aliens, and unicorns into the same conceptual thought experiment—<strong>skip that section entirely!</strong></p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="house-rules-for-discussions-feedback" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="house-rules-for-discussions-feedback">House Rules for Discussions &amp; Feedback</h3>
<p>You are warmly invited to comment, debate, and constructively critique any post. To keep this space constructive and enjoyable to read, please adhere to a single cardinal rule:</p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p><strong>Ad hominem attacks are strictly forbidden. Ideas, however, can be attacked as fiercely and relentlessly as you wish.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Bring your sharpest arguments, not your personal grievances.</p>
<hr>
</section>
<section id="the-road-ahead" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-road-ahead">The Road Ahead</h3>
<p>It is important to emphasize that the work presented across these notes remains firmly in the theoretical realm. While these concepts are explored with analytical depth, their real-world physical implementation will demand significant experimental and technological breakthroughs. Scaling and testing them will inevitably reveal growing layers of complexity, demanding both deep interdisciplinary synergy and sustained reflection.</p>
<p>And yet, despite all the underlying complexity… the fundamental rules are so strikingly intuitive that a five-year-old could grasp them without breaking a sweat.</p>
<p>👉 <strong>Quick start:</strong><br>
<strong><a href="posts/quick-intro/index.qmd">“Quantum Molecular Computing Explained to a 5 Year Old”</a></strong></p>
<hr>
<p>👉 <strong>Ready to learn the theory? Click here:</strong><br>
<strong><a href="posts/boolean-logic/index.qmd">“A Refresher on Boolean Logic”</a></strong></p>


</section>

 ]]></description>
